title: A rule-regex refusal is reported under a leaf path it has nothing to do with
updated: 2026-08-22T16:03:43Z
---

karr #161 raises the rule-dialect refusal where the rule is USED, which for `encrypt` is inside the leaf walk. `File::SOPS::_assert_leaves_representable` asks should_encrypt_path inside an eval and re-throws with `_at_path`, so the message comes out as:

    foo: Cannot use '(?=f)foo' as the unencrypted_regex: it uses a lookahead ...

The `foo:` is the first leaf the walk reached and has nothing to do with the
problem, which is the rule. It reads as though the KEY were at fault.

rotate and edit are unaffected -- they reach should_encrypt_path outside that
eval and the message comes out clean (verified).

Two ways out, neither in Metadata.pm:
 - have `_assert_leaves_representable` not wrap an exception that did not come
   from assert_representable, or
 - validate the rule once in File::SOPS::_metadata_for (or wherever the
   Metadata is built for a write) so the refusal is raised before any walk.

The second is the better one and is also where a nicer message could name the
argument the caller passed. Both are in lib/File/SOPS.pm, which was outside
karr #161 file boundary.

NOT a candidate: validating in Metadata BUILD/from_hash. That would refuse to
READ a document carrying such a rule, and decrypt/extract deliberately still
work on one (verified against a document sops wrote with
--unencrypted-regex '(?=f)foo').

2026-08-22 04:59 Closed as a side effect of docs/adr/0051, not as a patch: the rule is checked once in _assert_rules_supported when the metadata for a write is built, so there is no leaf path left for the message to be reported under. Took option 2 from the ticket body (validate where the Metadata is built), which the ticket itself preferred. Pinned in t/66 section 3, for both regex fields and for a Perl-uncompilable pattern.

REVIEW (Hauptagent, 2026-08-22): die Meldung selbst provoziert. Sie beginnt jetzt mit "Cannot use \x27(?=foo)\x27 as the unencrypted_regex: ..." -- kein vorangestellter Leaf-Pfad mehr. assert_rule_regexes_agree wird an drei Stellen vor jedem Walk gerufen (SOPS.pm:1653, 1831, 2951), also gibt es keinen Leaf-Pfad, unter dem die Refusal noch auftauchen koennte.
